WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.840
About a year ago, I released a video called&nbsp;
“The Problem with Budget Cards”. To this day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.880
that particular video is my longest video and also&nbsp;
my second best performing video in terms of views.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:17.560
It’s also wildly unpopular compared to literally&nbsp;
everything else I’ve released. The like to dislike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:24.360
proportion is an abysmal 77%—for context, my&nbsp;
second lowest is around 95%—and a brief look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.000
at the comments section indicates that a lot of&nbsp;
people take issue with various things contained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:35.160
within the video. I’ve asked myself a number of&nbsp;
times if I should do something with that video—is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.640
the video truly enough of a departure from the&nbsp;
quality of the rest of my work to warrant some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:45.240
sort of action, a note in the description or a&nbsp;
pinned comment saying that I, enlightened future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:50.760
Alex, disavow the contents of the video? And up&nbsp;
until now, I would always come to the conclusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:56.760
that the answer is no. My running narrative to&nbsp;
myself has been that it’s a largely fine video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.040
and that the reason people reacted the way they&nbsp;
did comes down to some poor wording here and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.400
there along with my choice to frontload the most&nbsp;
controversial argument in the video. I figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.560
this was likely amplified by an especially saucy&nbsp;
thumbnail, along with slow pacing and the droning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.400
sluggish delivery that characterized my&nbsp;
first three videos. However, I haven’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.120
really put much thought into the arguments&nbsp;
themselves, and now that a year has passed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:27.480
I want to do just that. I am here revisiting that&nbsp;
video, along with its central question—“Are cheap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:34.920
powerful cards a problem?” Was I onto something,&nbsp;
or flat out wrong, or somewhere in between?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.200
In order to answer that, I’m going to first&nbsp;
summarize what that video is saying and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:45.305
can skip three minutes ahead if you’re already&nbsp;
familiar with the contents of that video.

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:45.320
[clears throat]

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.920
Sol Ring is a powerful mana artifact&nbsp;
printed very early in Magic’s history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.080
It was banned in Legacy and restricted&nbsp;
in Vintage, leading to it commanding a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.320
moderate-but affordable price for players in&nbsp;
the lightly played casual format of commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.720
which was slowly gaining steam during the 2000s.&nbsp;
Due to some players considering it a staple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.320
it ended up being printed in all 5 decks in&nbsp;
the first commander set in 2011. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.480
it being a staple is a flawed argument for&nbsp;
reprinting it, because the card was only a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:11.840
staple due to its affordable price, itself a&nbsp;
result of it not being legal in a large format.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.240
Printing a set for commander would naturally&nbsp;
grow the format, grow the demand for Sol Ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.320
and without a reprint the card’s price would be&nbsp;
pushed in line with similar fast mana cards such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:23.480
as Manacrypt, rendering it likewise a tool for&nbsp;
the power-gamers. I believe the format would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:27.440
better if things had played out this way. Some&nbsp;
have argued it should be banned, but that ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.200
has sailed given the number of reprints it&nbsp;
has seen. However, Sol Ring still poses an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.040
interesting question—what is the effect of cards&nbsp;
with wildly mismatched price and power level?

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.800
For one, they warp how players look at other&nbsp;
cards, and this affects how many of a given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.880
type of card players will run. Some players might&nbsp;
decide how many cards of a type to run ahead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:48.360
time, but most won’t. Instead, players will add&nbsp;
the best options within their budget, and then a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:52.120
lot of them will notice the rapidly worsening&nbsp;
power-to-money ratio and stop adding them at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.800
certain point, even if running more of that type&nbsp;
of card would be better for their deck. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.320
vegetables like interaction and ramp, it’s easy&nbsp;
to distinguish better options vs worse options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.880
but when you get to fuzzier, more synergistic&nbsp;
cards it’s more difficult to distinguish card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:07.560
quality because analyzing them often requires&nbsp;
thinking about a variety of other cards and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.920
different situations. This leads to players adding&nbsp;
a handful of the most powerful vegetable staples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.040
and then overloading on synergistic cards, leading&nbsp;
to decks that are crammed with win-more cards but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:19.840
lack a lot of the basic building blocks that&nbsp;
lead to a deck performing well consistently.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.240
In addition to cards that pack a high power&nbsp;
level compared to their price, a related type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:26.920
of card that can cause problems is expensive&nbsp;
staples that are already in one’s collection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.720
and which are thus free to add. As an example,&nbsp;
in my mid-power Meria Voltron deck I initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.960
added Sword of Forge and Frontier, but ended&nbsp;
up cutting it as it felt out of place. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:39.760
pumped up the deck’s power level, but without&nbsp;
solving any of the deck’s existing weaknesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:44.320
making the deck less well-rounded overall. As&nbsp;
another example, there are a lot of very powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:48.680
graveyard synergies out there, but the game is&nbsp;
also filled with extremely powerful silver-bullet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.560
graveyard hate. This means that upgrading&nbsp;
a mid-power graveyard deck can backfire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.120
putting it in competition with decks that will&nbsp;
likely be running cards with the capability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.920
totally dismantle an unprepared graveyard&nbsp;
deck, and leading to a less consistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.320
play experience. As another example, choosing to&nbsp;
run infinite combos or other powerful strategies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.120
will draw attacks and interaction from opponents&nbsp;
who are in the know, and this can be a problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:11.760
if a player isn’t prepared to deal with this&nbsp;
hate. If your deck has a wide range of power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.800
levels in its potential plays, opponents will&nbsp;
often be wary and default to attacking you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:19.760
even if the average power level of your deck&nbsp;
is lower than that of some opposing decks.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.960
Conclusion. Sol Ring is upsettingly powerful but&nbsp;
isn’t going to get banned. You should figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.640
the correct number of things you need in&nbsp;
a particular category ahead of time, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:29.960
avoid loading up too hard on synergistic win-more&nbsp;
stuff. Sometimes the theoretically most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.320
option out there won’t be the most powerful option&nbsp;
for your deck, and sometimes the most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:37.360
option for your deck won’t be the best idea for&nbsp;
the type of games you want to play. I don’t run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.960
Torment of Hailfire in my turbo-ramping Glissa&nbsp;
deck because I don’t want my opponents to equate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:46.520
me having a lot of lands with an immediate game&nbsp;
loss. Queue time machine gag, the video is over.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:50.480
Okay, that is The Problem with Budget Cards,&nbsp;
condensed down into a pithy three minute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:54.360
summary. With a year of additional&nbsp;
experience in writing, video making,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:59.320
and playing commander, what do I think about this&nbsp;
video’s arguments? Well, past the four minute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:04.680
introduction they’re mostly fine, if a little&nbsp;
unfocused. The second section of the video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:09.600
where I talk about the effect of other cards&nbsp;
being framed negatively by cheap powerful cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:13.920
I maybe overstate the social dynamics at play&nbsp;
a little bit, as I had the tendency to do in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:19.320
some of my earlier videos. However, a lot of my&nbsp;
theorizing here does line up with observations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:24.560
I’ve had playing commander and looking at&nbsp;
decklists—people do tend to overrate synergistic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:30.520
escalation-type cards and underrate some of the&nbsp;
more generic vegetables of the world. Players are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:35.440
clearly better at distinguishing power levels of&nbsp;
simpler cards as compared to more complex ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.480
and this affects what their decks look like. Now,&nbsp;
this point shouldn’t take 7 minutes to make and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:45.280
don’t think this section is written as well as it&nbsp;
could be, but the point itself is basically fine.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.720
The third section of the video is what I would&nbsp;
consider to be the core of this video, and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:53.880
basically a collection of somewhat-related&nbsp;
arguments branching off from the concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:58.560
of cards in a deck having diverse power levels.&nbsp;
All of these arguments are individually pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:04.440
good IMHO, and you’ll recognize elements of them&nbsp;
repackaged into later videos I’ve made. Choosing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:08.560
not to put powerful staples into mid-power decks&nbsp;
is something that I basically made an entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:12.840
video about, and I’ve likewise talked about the&nbsp;
problem with adding random infinite combos to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:18.040
deck. Deck consistency is also a subject I’ve&nbsp;
talked about numerous times in the past year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:22.680
and it’s a core pillar of this section of&nbsp;
the video. Even when I briefly jump back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:27.240
into talking about Sol Ring near the conclusion&nbsp;
of the video, it’s at this point decently framed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.960
and I think the argument I make in this section&nbsp;
about the card is a solid one. Having variable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:37.200
power levels of the cards in your deck can lead to&nbsp;
some unintended problems, and while this isn’t a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:41.440
blistering indictment of Sol Ring in particular,&nbsp;
it at least makes sense how Sol Ring might fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:45.560
into this sort of argumentative framework.&nbsp;
The conclusion to the video basically just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:49.960
restates a lot of the points in the previous&nbsp;
two sections, and seeing how I’m okay with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:55.000
most of these points individually, it’s likewise&nbsp;
fine. I don’t think the video does a great job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.760
of connecting the various points within it, but&nbsp;
all of them at least follow to some degree from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.600
the core premise of the video, even if that&nbsp;
premise is made a little murky at times.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:09.600
With the later parts out of the way, let’s get to&nbsp;
the introduction. The four minute intro is easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.080
the most controversial part of the video, and&nbsp;
probably the part that ended up causing the lion’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:21.040
share of the negative reaction the video received.&nbsp;
So, how do I feel about those arguments now? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:26.680
the rhetoric is pretty bad—I didn’t argue the&nbsp;
points well. Above all else this is a structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:33.160
issue—I simply don’t really articulate what my&nbsp;
problem with Sol Ring is in this section. Why is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:37.760
it a problem for an extremely powerful card&nbsp;
to exist among much weaker cards in a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:43.720
portion of EDH decks? I cover that territory in a&nbsp;
roundabout way later in the video, but that’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:49.600
good enough for a 22 minute YouTube video. Like&nbsp;
sure, if somebody watches the entire video and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:54.440
evaluates it cohesively they might have a decent&nbsp;
chance of catching on to what I’m going for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:58.720
but look at this goddamn graph—less than half&nbsp;
of people are still watching when I get to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:03.600
part of the video where I start to justify my&nbsp;
Sol Ring slander. Now, it’s not objectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.920
bad argumentation to lead with a controversial&nbsp;
claim to draw people in and give them a point of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:13.800
focus—that can be kinda cool actually—but this&nbsp;
video isn’t set up for that sort of rhetorical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:19.080
style. It makes a bold argument—that Sol Ring&nbsp;
shouldn’t have been reprinted—with little support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:23.680
or fanfare, and not much of a framing device with&nbsp;
which to guide the audience to the justifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:29.160
points. This is exacerbated by the slow pacing&nbsp;
and bloated runtime of the second section of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:34.840
video, as it likely burns off any sort of tension&nbsp;
or momentum that a controversial argument might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:40.200
garner. This structuring obviously isn’t good for&nbsp;
the reality of people watching YouTube videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:44.320
and it’s weak even for the people who fully&nbsp;
watched the video and charitably interpreted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:49.560
its contents, as many commenters did. The points&nbsp;
about Sol Ring simply aren’t structured in a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:53.800
way to persuade people who don’t already&nbsp;
agree with my conclusions on some level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.880
and the fact that Sol Ring was then&nbsp;
the framing device for the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:01.800
the video predictably caused additional&nbsp;
problems in maintaining a cohesive focus.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:06.440
So that’s it, right? Future enlightened Snail&nbsp;
has decreed that the intro to The Problem with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:12.800
Budget Cards is bad. Well, no. I mean it’s&nbsp;
not good, let’s be plenty clear about that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:19.080
but my views haven’t really changed on the points&nbsp;
I was trying to make. I don’t think Sol Ring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:23.320
should have been reprinted in commander sets, and&nbsp;
that’s because I don’t think fast mana belongs in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:29.040
casual commander. I don’t have some crazy essay&nbsp;
worth of stuff to say in defense of that opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:33.920
I just don’t like what explosive starts do&nbsp;
to the tempo of games. This is the argument I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.000
should have led with in that video if I even&nbsp;
wanted to talk about Sol Ring, because it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:42.600
extremely simple and impossible to misinterpret&nbsp;
for somebody who isn’t fully dedicated to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.680
task of misinterpreting it. If you disagree that&nbsp;
fast mana doesn’t belong in casual commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:51.960
that's fine—there are valid reasons to disagree&nbsp;
with my thinking here, and I’m not here to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:58.000
your mind on that today. I do, however, want to&nbsp;
elaborate a little on why I think not printing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.920
Sol Ring in commander sets would aid in the goal&nbsp;
of keeping fast mana out of casual commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:06.320
because while I think a lot of people can&nbsp;
follow the chain of logic without me explaining,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:13.200
it’s not entirely trivial. To start with, Sol&nbsp;
Ring is fast mana—a term which generally refers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:17.920
to cards that are mana positive. Rituals can be&nbsp;
a bit of a grey zone—I generally include them if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:23.360
they cost no mana—but any repeatably-tapping mana&nbsp;
rock that is mana positive the turn it’s played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:28.880
is indisputably fast mana. These cards are the&nbsp;
most distinguishing feature of cEDH, the thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:35.600
that makes turn 1 or 2 wins even possible, and&nbsp;
Sol Ring fits into this category of card to a T.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:40.320
With the card’s fast mana status in mind, printing&nbsp;
Sol Ring in a precon aimed at getting people into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:47.000
the format has symbolic power, situating the card&nbsp;
as a casual staple that people should be playing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:51.480
The fact that it was printed in all five of&nbsp;
the first round of precons is all but saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:57.040
“dear casual commander players, this card goes&nbsp;
in your deck,” and it being a staple isn’t tied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:03.360
to any sort of competitive, keeping up aspect of&nbsp;
things—mostly, more on that later. I gestured at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:08.080
this argument when I pointed out that the first&nbsp;
round of precons were quite poorly constructed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:13.080
but I’ll make it explicit here—the decision&nbsp;
to print Sol Ring in every single precon since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:18.080
2011 has likely had several different reasons&nbsp;
behind it, and even if there was a primary one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:23.400
it wouldn’t be this sort of “keeping up” idea.&nbsp;
Commander isn’t a competitive format, there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:29.640
nothing that inherently requires “keeping up”&nbsp;
with. Rather, the scarce evidence that does exist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:34.240
suggests that Sol Ring was reprinted to&nbsp;
amplify its position as a symbol of the format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:37.920
to turn it into a mascot of sorts&nbsp;
that would be run far and wide and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:43.240
be a point of connectivity between basically&nbsp;
all players. Even though I dislike the card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:47.440
I can’t deny there’s a certain power in that,&nbsp;
a power that was recognized by Wizards of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:53.760
Coast in 2010. I also alluded to the monetary&nbsp;
motivation in The Problem with Budget Cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:58.080
and this is almost certainly another one of the&nbsp;
factors involved in printing Sol Ring. Putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:03.080
a piece of fast mana in every precon absolutely&nbsp;
helped Wizards of the Coast sell more units—they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:07.520
were a business putting out a new product line&nbsp;
and they wanted to make sure it was successful. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:12.840
was a bit smarmy about this a year ago, because&nbsp;
it’s very fun to be smarmy in YouTube videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:17.200
but the monetary element is kind of just&nbsp;
a fact of life in the world we live in.

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:22.280
Speaking of which, let’s talk about the price&nbsp;
argument. Heavily printing Sol Ring in commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:28.320
sets has undoubtedly kept the price of the card&nbsp;
low, and the price of a card being low leads more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:36.240
players to play it in casual EDH. A fast mana&nbsp;
card being $100 or $200 rather than $1 or $2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:43.120
will absolutely result in fewer people playing&nbsp;
that card. 85% of decks run Sol Ring, but only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:49.640
11% of players run the very similar Mana Crypt,&nbsp;
and every commander with a more than 50% play rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:58.440
of Mana Crypt is almost exclusively used in cEDH.&nbsp;
So, if my goal is less fast mana in casual EDH,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:03.920
and that is my general preference, not reprinting&nbsp;
Sol Ring absolutely would have accomplished that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:08.800
goal to a large extent. Now, this is allowed&nbsp;
to make you uncomfortable, and it makes me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:13.080
uncomfortable too, so let’s just take a minute&nbsp;
to sit in our discomfort with playing a card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:18.040
game that is also a network of commercialized&nbsp;
products. The whole thing feels kinda weird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:23.360
huh? Okay, getting back into the topic at&nbsp;
hand, I would posit that the argument I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:29.360
presented is more or less just a mixture of my&nbsp;
preferences and a set of readily available facts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:35.880
about the nature of humans, money, and markets.&nbsp;
However, on a vibes level I understand why this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:39.960
was a volcanically controversial take and&nbsp;
in retrospect I probably wouldn’t choose to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:45.480
make it again. A direct implication of what I’m&nbsp;
saying here is that I think it’s good when cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:50.920
I dislike are more expensive to keep them in fewer&nbsp;
people’s reach, and there’s something kinda harsh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:56.640
about that, right? There is an undeniable element&nbsp;
of inherent classism to players with more money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.640 --> 00:14:02.120
having access to more cards than less wealthy&nbsp;
opponents. This is most obvious when thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:07.400
about high-powered staples—as somebody who plays&nbsp;
mostly budget decks, you can believe that I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:12.000
experienced some fleeting pangs of frustration&nbsp;
when my tuned budget piles have gotten blown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:17.760
out by an opponent playing a series of powerful&nbsp;
staples that I’m unable to keep up with. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:22.520
if I may be allowed this one indulgence, I&nbsp;
want to give just a bit of pushback here.

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:27.400
The classism argument is a lot weaker when it&nbsp;
comes to EDH compared to basically every other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:33.080
constructed format in the game. cEDH is very&nbsp;
proxy-friendly, and for the rest of the EDH&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:39.160
format—the casual EDH format—there is no set&nbsp;
power level to keep up with. Lacking access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:44.240
a particular powerful card, while potentially&nbsp;
being a subjective issue within the context of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:51.160
a playgroup or table, doesn’t objectively make a&nbsp;
deck more or less ideal in any sense. Now, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:56.240
isn’t to say power level is irrelevant—players&nbsp;
might have a specific power level they want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.240 --> 00:15:01.080
play at, and if they’re struggling to boost a&nbsp;
deck up to that point a natural reaction might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:07.280
be to forlornly gaze upon the Rhystic Studies and&nbsp;
the Great Henges and the One Rings of the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:11.920
and ask why they must be denied these things&nbsp;
on account of their wallet. But the solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:17.040
of all players living in a land of ubiquitous&nbsp;
staples, with 25% of a deck immediately gobbled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:21.680
up by these sorts of high-powered cards as&nbsp;
soon as a player picks their colors—well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:28.040
it’s garbage. There’s a profound uncreativity to&nbsp;
the proliferation of so-called must-play staples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:32.800
within the commander format, and when I hear&nbsp;
players pine after this vision of the format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:37.920
my more callous instincts want to interpret this&nbsp;
as them saying “why can’t I be allowed to be less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:42.720
creative?” I certainly don’t want to discount&nbsp;
the feelings of players who are struggling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:48.400
win games and not having fun because of&nbsp;
it—those people exist and they matter—but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:52.960
in a casual social format I can come up with&nbsp;
several far better solutions to this problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:57.040
than just printing enough copies of The One&nbsp;
Ring that everybody and their uncle can have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.040 --> 00:16:03.000
one for 50 cents. If you want to play at a higher&nbsp;
power level, have a conversation about proxying!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:07.560
If you want to play at a lower power level,&nbsp;
talk to your opponents about that! And if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:12.200
people you find yourself playing against are too&nbsp;
ornery and inconsiderate to spend even an ounce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:17.980
of effort helping you have fun in a casual card&nbsp;
game format, they’re not worth playing against.

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:19.560
[pause]

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:24.120
I suppose this video wouldn’t be complete if&nbsp;
I didn’t return to the actual question of “The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:30.360
Problem with Budget Cards”—is there a problem with&nbsp;
budget cards? Is it bad when a card’s price in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:36.000
Dollars or Euros or Yen doesn’t reflect its power&nbsp;
level? Well, I think this is a question with a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:42.120
of layers to it, and my feelings were complicated&nbsp;
even a year ago—despite the title/thumbnail combo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:47.680
the actual contents of the video were more of a&nbsp;
free-form ramble on the subject of cards like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:53.720
rather than being some sort of pointed takedown.&nbsp;
Sol Ring in particular serves as the most dramatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:59.400
example of a power/cost imbalance, and the fact&nbsp;
that one of the most powerful mana artifacts in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:04.280
the game is run in all sorts of decks in the&nbsp;
range of janky and budget is a striking thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:11.320
to observe. This out-of-placeness is what I was&nbsp;
really trying to comment on—what does it mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.320 --> 00:17:16.640
when a Sol Ring sits in a deck alongside&nbsp;
a bunch of janky commons? To some people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:24.400
this situation constitutes a problem, but to&nbsp;
others the answer is a dynamic game. Sol Ring can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:29.520
be a factor that adds variance through its power&nbsp;
level and enables the common jank of the world to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:34.640
keep up with the sweaty, optimized piles of the&nbsp;
oligarchs of the Magic: the Gathering community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:39.640
some might say. To put it in more mathematical&nbsp;
terms, an injection of variance will always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:44.960
serve as an equalizing factoring within games.&nbsp;
This is true in terms of skill—a new player to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:49.440
monopoly will have a better chance against average&nbsp;
opponents than will a new player to chess—and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:54.040
can see a parallel to this in the fact that it’s&nbsp;
equally likely for any player at a table to draw a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:59.400
Sol Ring. We can also think about how Sol Ring&nbsp;
affects the average power level of a deck—if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:04.760
we construct a ten point power scale and put Sol&nbsp;
Ring at a 10 out of 10, a deck whose average power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:09.980
level is a 4 out of 10 will be more improved by&nbsp;
a Sol Ring than a deck whose power level is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:15.080
8 out of 10. Thus, you could argue that Sol&nbsp;
Ring and cards that are similarly accessible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:20.120
and powerful serve as a moderating factor&nbsp;
between different decks and power levels.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:25.280
I don’t find this argument particularly compelling&nbsp;
as a justification for Sol Ring itself, such is my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:29.800
distaste for fast mana, but the broader point is&nbsp;
a reasonable one that speaks to the complexity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:34.480
of a casual card game format. In addition,&nbsp;
there will always be cards that are powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:39.600
compared to their price—cards see varying numbers&nbsp;
of copies printed, and there are a myriad of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:45.920
factors that can also serve to pump up or depress&nbsp;
a given card’s price. So, with all that in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:50.080
the answer to the question “Is there a&nbsp;
problem with budget cards?” is that no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:55.200
there is absolutely no inherent problem&nbsp;
with powerful cards being cheap. Rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:58.840
prices of cards have an influence on&nbsp;
power level variance, and within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.840 --> 00:19:03.680
context of a social format like EDH, the&nbsp;
cards being varying power levels poses certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:09.080
challenges to players. Power level differences&nbsp;
in cards are more easily detectable in simpler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:13.680
more mundane cards than they are for flashy&nbsp;
synergy picks, and players should be careful to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:18.240
make sure that they end up with the right balance&nbsp;
of vegetables to other stuff. If your deck has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:23.280
mixture of stronger and weaker cards, this will&nbsp;
naturally increase its variance, and in some cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:28.160
that added variance might make opponents wary. If&nbsp;
they’ve seen your deck’s pop off scenario before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:31.640
they might focus attention on you even if&nbsp;
there’s another deck at the table that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:37.400
more powerful overall but lower variance. These&nbsp;
are all challenges posed by cards having variable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:43.400
power levels, and navigating them can help players&nbsp;
to have more fun. The framing device of budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:48.000
cards was the one I chose to string together the&nbsp;
specific set of points I wanted to make a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:53.160
ago, but the questions at the heart of the video&nbsp;
run much deeper than money. Casual formats in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:57.560
sort of game will always have an inherent tension&nbsp;
between more competitive play and play that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.560 --> 00:20:03.160
more expressive and less goal oriented. These&nbsp;
things exist on a hazy spectrum, and there’s no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:07.920
one place on that spectrum that will maximize fun&nbsp;
for all people involved in a given game or format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:13.160
no single right answer to questions about how&nbsp;
decks should or shouldn’t be put together. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:19.360
the end of the day, I’m just one guy giving you my&nbsp;
best advice on how to navigate that complex world.

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:23.360
Thanks for making it to the end! This was&nbsp;
a bit different than most of what I make,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:26.840
and you can let me know what you thought&nbsp;
about it, because I’m genuinely curious if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.840 --> 00:20:31.320
this retreading of a controversial topic worked&nbsp;
any better for people. If you want to support me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:36.160
in making videos like this, you can subscribe to&nbsp;
my Patreon for $3 a month. I’m currently taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:38.760
submissions for my August deck review there if&nbsp;
that is of any interest to you, and I’ve also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:45.520
started releasing less-scripted bonus videos there&nbsp;
as well, including one about cascade a week ago.

